[{"role": "system", "content": "You maintain `skill.md`, a short, general reference of design lessons for an automated process that's evolving a population of solitaire game variants (written in a custom format called SGDL) generation by generation.\n\nEach generation, many independently-running variants are each given exactly one focused revision based on their own simulated playtest results, by a separate process that has no visibility into what any of the other variants are doing. Whoever makes each revision also writes a short \"insight\": a general, transferable lesson suggested by that one specific change and its outcome, not a narration of what they did. You'll be given the current skill.md (or told it doesn't exist yet, if this is the first generation) plus the full batch of insights produced this generation, and your job is to produce the next version of skill.md.\n\nTreat skill.md as a living document you rewrite each time, not a log you append to:\n\n- Integrate new insights into the existing structure rather than tacking them on. If several insights from this generation (or an insight and an existing point) are really saying the same thing in different words, merge them into one clearer, possibly stronger statement -- don't keep near-duplicates as separate bullets.\n- If this generation's evidence contradicts or weakens an existing point, revise or remove it rather than letting both versions stand.\n- Look for what's well-supported. If most of this generation's relevant insights agree, that's worth keeping or strengthening; if only one or two insights suggest something that doesn't fit with everything else, it may just be noise from one particular variant rather than a real general lesson, and it's fine to leave out.\n- Write every point as a general principle about solitaire design, not as a fact about one specific file's exact numbers (\"foundation piles that no move rule ever targets sit permanently empty regardless of other changes\" is useful; \"Trizug's draw pile of 24 cards was too small\" is not, since nobody will recognize \"Trizug\" later).\n- Organize the document under a small number of clear headers (e.g. draw pile design, difficulty and winnability, avoiding unused elements, win conditions), and keep the whole thing short enough that it's actually useful as a quick reference -- a sprawling document nobody can read in full defeats the purpose. A specific cap on total bullet points is given to you below; treat it as a hard limit, not a target to approach.\n- It's completely fine for this to be a light touch-up most generations and a more substantial rewrite occasionally, depending on what the evidence actually supports.\n\nEnd your response with the complete new skill.md -- the whole document, not a diff -- in a single fenced code block tagged `skill`, as the last thing in your response. You can reason about what to keep, merge, or change beforehand.\n"}, {"role": "user", "content": "Current skill.md:\n\n```skill\n# skill.md\n\n## Establish a valid, playable core\n- Treat compile failures, bad deck accounting, invalid references, impossible predicates, and overlapping move-definition conflicts as first-order design failures. Fix the smallest structural issue that restores a runnable game before tuning difficulty.\n- Prefer minimal, informative repairs: preserve the current pile roles and move graph where possible, restore exact card totals, and re-check that every rule reference and win-condition target is valid in context.\n- Validate rule/setup compatibility early. Build and opening predicates must be realizable by the actual deck and layout; duplicated decks, reduced decks, or collapsed color distributions can turn standard-looking relations into impossible or overly loose rules.\n- Repair the move graph before balancing challenge. Universal 0-move starts, near-zero usage, or tiny exhausted searches usually mean active piles lack a broadly available opening transfer or cannot reach any real progress route.\n\n## Align card flow, sinks, and victory\n- If the win condition requires active piles to empty, the design needs at least one reachable irreversible sink that the objective explicitly rewards. Rearrangement alone cannot satisfy an emptying goal.\n- Sink capacity must match the deck and objective. Too few destinations, too little parallelism, duplicated suit-runs without duplicated sink structure, or over-narrow opening rules can make a game mathematically unwinnable even when local mobility looks rich.\n- Prefer incremental, steady progress sinks over all-at-once completion gates. Designs are more solvable when ordinary exposed cards can convert organization into durable advancement step by step.\n- If a pile can receive cards but not release them, it should represent completion, not temporary storage that the game later expects to empty. One-way receiving piles that are not part of victory usually become dead sinks, blockers, or search noise.\n- When a one-way receiving or accumulation area already exists, first consider aligning the win condition to that existing progress route or repurposing it into the main sink before adding a new subsystem.\n- Keep progress irreversible when possible. Easy backflow out of foundations or other sink piles often turns advancement into churn and makes search harder without adding meaningful decisions.\n- Every populated pile type should have a clear role in the win path: expose cards, reorganize them, or accumulate irreversible progress.\n\n## Shape mobility and search space\n- Distinguish deadness from churn. Near-zero use points to missing opening contact or a broken progress path; search-cap losses with moderate-to-high use usually point to excessive reversible freedom or insufficient irreversible progress.\n- When movement is plentiful but wins are absent, improve the progress route before adding more mobility. Expanding sink access, capacity, or incrementality usually helps more than adding extra tableau freedom.\n- When search is broad but progress already exists, tighten the busiest reversible routes before adding new ones. Empty-space reuse, permissive helper piles, and broad tableau-to-tableau transfers are common sources of churn.\n- Favor movement rules that reinforce the game\u2019s main structure. Tableau relations and stack moves should organize cards in the same direction that the sink rewards, not create free circulation.\n- Broad holding areas are risky. Helper piles should either be genuinely temporary and clearable, or be converted into constrained completion zones.\n\n## Tune openings and pacing with small, compatible changes\n- For frequent 0- or 1-move losses in otherwise workable games, add the narrowest broadly available opening rule that fits the design: a simple tableau relation, a small increase in starting exposure, or a rank-limited move to empties.\n- Prefer constrained empty-pile rules over unrestricted ones. Rank-gated or single-card access to empties often rescues dead starts while preserving planning value and avoiding degenerate shuffling.\n- Small layout shifts can matter when rules are sound: a slight increase in tableau occupancy, starting exposure, or stock feed can improve early contact without flattening the game\u2019s core decisions.\n- If losses split between immediate dead starts and long search churn, suspect \u201crearrangement without resolution\u201d: improve both early contact and the sink\u2019s ability to turn motion into permanent progress.\n\n## Express rules cleanly\n- Keep move definitions compact and non-overlapping. If the engine rejects duplicate source/destination rules, merge related permissions into one coherent condition tree instead of scattering near-duplicates.\n- Use syntax and compile fixes to clarify structure, not just to silence errors: after every repair, verify that the resulting rules still form one coherent source \u2192 tableau/workspace \u2192 sink progression.\n```\n\nThis generation produced 100 insight(s) from independently revised variants:\n\n1. When a solitaire shows high move counts and broad pile/card usage but no wins, the usual problem is not lack of mobility but lack of a clean irreversible sink. Tightening the sink so it rewards one clear progression often improves solvability more than adding yet more rearrangement freedom.\n2. When a variant fails to compile because not all cards are placed, the best next step is usually the smallest count correction that preserves the current pile roles and move graph. Restore a runnable baseline before changing difficulty or adding new mechanics.\n3. When a game is losing in fully exhausted searches after only a handful of moves, first check for a structural contradiction between sinks and the win condition: a one-way receiving pile that is not counted as progress can make the game unwinnable regardless of shuffle. In reduced-suit decks, also re-evaluate color-based tableau rules, since standard predicates may become either trivial or too restrictive.\n4. When a game uses duplicated suits or multiple deck copies, the number of completion/sink piles must scale with that duplication; otherwise locally valid organization can still lead to globally impossible deals. If some losses are immediate dead starts while others are fully explored unwinnables, a small increase in sink capacity can fix both better than adding more reversible movement.\n5. When a solitaire is proving unwinnable in tiny exhausted searches, first check whether the progress sink is reachable at all from the opening layout. Increasing starting exposure is a minimal way to test whether the core move graph works before redesigning deeper mechanics.\n6. When a deal-type stock feeds tableau piles but the win condition requires those piles to empty, the stock usually also needs to participate directly in play; otherwise it acts more like delayed blockage than like meaningful pacing. If a game shows both dead starts and long unwinnable searches, first connect the stock to the main progress routes before adding more tableau freedom.\n7. When a solitaire has plenty of legal motion but no wins, first check whether its irreversible sink has enough total capacity and granularity to absorb the whole deck under the actual deck multiplicity. Also, unrestricted empty holding cells often create search churn; rank-gating empty fills can preserve choices while making temporary space more purposeful.\n8. When a solitaire already contains a one-way accumulation pile, first consider making that pile the objective rather than adding more mobility elsewhere. Long search-cap losses with decent card usage often mean the game has rearrangement but no rewarded endpoint; aligning the win condition to an existing irreversible sink can convert churn into progress with a minimal rules change.\n9. When a reduced or duplicated deck contains multiple copies of the same suit, foundation capacity has to scale with that duplication if victory depends on emptying the working piles. An otherwise reasonable build-and-foundation structure can be mathematically unwinnable simply because the sink can absorb only one copy of each suit sequence.\n10. When a game uses suit-matched ascending foundations as its main sink, foundation count has to match the number of distinct suit sequences the deck can produce. In multi-deck games, too few foundation piles can make the objective mathematically unreachable even if local movement looks rich.\n11. When a solitaire already has a one-way accumulation area, the first repair for universal unwinnability is often to align victory with that existing sink rather than adding more reshuffling. Also, if some deals start with no legal move at all, a small new workspace destination can be a better opening fix than broadly loosening every build rule.\n12. If a solitaire\u2019s win condition requires piles to empty, the design needs at least one destination that counts as completed progress rather than temporary storage. When playtesting shows lots of movement but proven unwinnable starts, repurposing an existing one-way accumulation area into a true sink is often a better first fix than adding more mobility.\n13. When a design already has one-way ascending suit piles, it's usually better to align victory with emptying the active piles into that existing sink than to require every sink pile to reach a fixed size. Win conditions should match total sink capacity and the actual flow of cards through the game.\n14. If the objective is to empty working piles, the game needs a reachable sink with enough capacity to absorb the deck incrementally; rearrangement plus a tiny holding cell is usually mathematically unwinnable. When a non-winning helper pile already exists, repurposing it into the main progress sink is often a cleaner fix than adding more mobility.\n15. If a solitaire already has a one-way receiving area, the first repair for universal long-search losses is often to align victory with that area and make its acceptance rule express clear, irreversible progress. Rearrangement without a rewarded sink creates churn; a foundation-like sink converts mobility into solvable structure.\n16. When a deck contains duplicated suit sequences, completion capacity has to scale with them. If the only irreversible sink accepts one full run per suit but the deck contains two copies, the game can become unwinnable or search-heavy even when local movement is rich.\n17. When a solitaire has lots of legal movement but no wins, first check whether any receiving pile actually converts motion into irreversible progress rewarded by the win condition. Reusing an existing pile as the true sink is often a smaller, clearer fix than adding more mobility or another subsystem.\n18. When a solitaire already wins cleanly on successful deals but some shuffles prove unwinnable after very short or low-usage searches, the next step is often to improve initial exposure rather than add more movement rules. A small increase in visible information can rescue dead openings without turning the whole game into unrestricted churn.\n19. When a solitaire has plenty of movement but no wins, first check whether its irreversible sink is actually aligned with the deck and build rules; duplicated or reduced-suit decks often need a sink structure that matches their natural sequencing rather than a borrowed standard foundation rule.\n\nSmall structural repairs should do double duty when possible: fix exact card accounting and strengthen the main progress route before adding more tableau mobility.\n20. When a deck duplicates suit-rank cards, completion piles often need to duplicate as well; otherwise a locally sensible foundation rule can become globally over-capacity or force the search to solve two runs through one sink. If play shows lots of movement but no wins, first check whether irreversible destination capacity actually matches the deck\u2019s multiplicity.\n21. When a game uses duplicated suit sets and wins by emptying active piles into ascending suit foundations, foundation count is capacity, not decoration: too few receiving piles can make many deals mathematically unwinnable even if movement rules are otherwise rich. Before adding mobility, make sure the irreversible sink can actually absorb the full deck structure the layout creates.\n22. When a game uses duplicated suits or multiple deck copies, sink capacity has to scale with that duplication too. If progress piles only support one copy of each suit-run but the deck contains two, apparent mobility can mask a structural bottleneck that causes both impossible deals and excessive search churn.\n23. When a game uses duplicated decks or repeated full suit runs, foundation capacity has to scale with that repetition; otherwise the tableau can generate lots of legal rearrangement without enough parallel sink space to convert it into a win. If simulations show high move counts with moderate card usage but zero wins, first strengthen irreversible progress before adding more mobility.\n24. When a game shows high pile usage but no wins, look for one-way receiving piles that absorb structure without contributing to victory. Making such piles re-enter the main build network often improves solvability more effectively than adding yet more free movement elsewhere.\n25. When a solitaire layout has many tableau/workspace moves but no wins, first check whether its \u201csink\u201d actually has enough capacity and the right build direction to absorb the whole deck. Incremental, irreversible foundations usually improve solvability more than adding yet more rearrangement moves; if needed, offset that by slightly constraining empty-space mobility rather than weakening the sink.\n26. When a variant has only one suit or otherwise reduced suit diversity, standard \u201cAce-up foundation\u201d assumptions can accidentally remove most of the deck from the win path. If the objective is to empty working piles, make sure there is a sink with enough reachable capacity to absorb the whole deck, and align victory with that sink rather than with pure rearrangement.\n27. When a deck contains many duplicate copies of the same suit, standard suit-ascending foundations can create hidden capacity problems even if they look familiar. In such cases, make the sink match the deck\u2019s duplication pattern\u2014e.g. parallel equal-rank accumulation or another incremental structure\u2014so ordinary progress is irreversible and mathematically sufficient for the whole deck.\n28. When a solitaire wins on every shuffle with very low variance, look first for an overly permissive sink rather than a lack of content. Tightening the irreversible progress route often adds meaningful planning while preserving mobility and card usage better than broadly restricting all movement.\n29. When a solitaire loses by hitting search limits with moderate card and pile usage, the problem is often not lack of mobility but mobility that does not align with permanent progress. Prefer tableau rules that organize cards in the same direction the sink rewards, so rearrangement naturally exposes and prepares cards for advancement instead of creating long reversible churn.\n30. When a solitaire file fails to compile because not all cards are placed, make the smallest count correction that preserves the intended pile roles before changing rules. You need a runnable baseline before you can tell whether difficulty comes from the move graph or just from broken setup arithmetic.\n31. When a solitaire uses suit-matched ascending foundations as its irreversible sink, the number of foundation piles should usually match the deck\u2019s natural completion structure. Extra sink piles can accidentally make the win condition impossible or create irrelevant space; too few can bottleneck progress just as badly.\n32. When a solitaire already has one-way accumulation piles, first align victory around them instead of adding more rearrangement. Long search-cap losses with decent pile usage often mean the game can move cards around but has no irreversible progress route the win condition actually rewards.\n33. When a game shows high pile usage and long non-exhaustive searches but no wins, the problem is often churn rather than immobility: cards can move, but not toward irreversible completion fast enough. In duplicated-deck games, foundation capacity and parallelism must scale with the amount of repeated suit material, or otherwise-valid building rules can become a bottleneck.\n34. When a solitaire shows high search activity but zero wins, look first for a missing incremental sink: if the only irreversible progress requires fully completed runs, the game often degenerates into endless rearrangement. Converting completion into stepwise foundation building can preserve the tableau\u2019s organizing logic while giving the search a real path to victory.\n35. When a layout includes more full suit-runs than its completion area can absorb, losses can be forced regardless of local mobility. Before tuning difficulty, make sure sink capacity and victory conditions match the deck\u2019s actual number of distinct runs, and prefer repurposing existing receiving piles into the main progress route over adding free rearrangement.\n36. When a solitaire\u2019s win condition is \u201cempty the tableau,\u201d its irreversible sinks must have enough total capacity to absorb the entire deck under the game\u2019s build rules. If sink capacity is mathematically short, no amount of tableau tuning can reliably fix solvability.\n37. When a solitaire uses multiple copies of a standard deck, completion sinks usually need to scale with that duplication too. If local movement is abundant but every run times out, check for a hidden capacity mismatch between the number of cards that must leave play and the number of piles that can permanently accept them.\n38. When a reduced or duplicated deck contains more complete suit-runs than your sink structure can absorb, unwinnability can be mathematical rather than strategic. Before tuning mobility, make sure irreversible destination capacity matches the number of runs the win condition implicitly asks the player to clear.\n39. When a solitaire variant has some full, healthy wins but many losses that exhaust after only a handful of moves, the bottleneck is often opening contact rather than overall sink capacity. A good fix is to widen single-card access to empty spaces while keeping multi-card or full-build moves more constrained, so dead starts improve without turning the whole game into unrestricted shuffling.\n40. When a solitaire already has a one-way receiving pile, the first repair for unwinnable \u201cempty the tableau\u201d objectives is often to align victory with that existing sink rather than adding more lateral movement. A game with rearrangement plus a non-winning sink tends to oscillate between dead starts and churn unless irreversible progress is explicitly what winning measures.\n41. When a solitaire already has a working sink but most losses hit search limits instead of exhausting quickly, the next improvement is often to reduce reversible tableau freedom rather than add more mobility. Also, sink parallelism should match deck structure: duplicated suits or multiple suit families usually need enough separate completion lanes to avoid turning valid progress into self-blocking congestion.\n42. When a solitaire shows high pile usage and repeatedly hits search limits without wins, the problem is often reversible motion without a strong completion route. Converting a loose receiving pile into a conventional, irreversible sink that advances in small steps can improve solvability more than adding yet more tableau mobility.\n43. When a game uses multiple copies of the same suits, completion capacity has to scale with that duplication. If victory asks the player to empty the tableau into suit-building sinks, too few sink piles can make the game effectively unwinnable even when local movement is abundant.\n44. When a solitaire file fails basic deck accounting, fix that with the smallest possible count adjustment before rebalancing rules; preserving the existing move graph makes later playtest results much easier to interpret.\n45. When a solitaire file won\u2019t compile, fix the exact structural defect with the smallest possible change before rebalancing. Preserving the existing pile roles and move graph makes the next round\u2019s playtest data far more informative than a simultaneous rules rewrite.\n46. When a solitaire already has a one-way receiving area, first align victory to that sink instead of adding more subsystems. Also, any populated pile type with no legal moves is usually a structural defect, not a balance issue; giving it a clear role often fixes both dead starts and unwinnability.\n47. When a deck contains duplicate copies of each suit, completion capacity has to scale with them: a foundation system sized for one copy of each suit can leave the game mathematically unable to absorb the full deck. In high-churn losses, increasing irreversible sink capacity is often a better first fix than adding more tableau mobility.\n48. When a game uses duplicated suits or multiple deck copies, completion sinks must scale with that duplication too; otherwise the layout may support plenty of rearrangement while the win condition remains mathematically unreachable. Before tuning mobility, verify that irreversible destination capacity can actually absorb every card the objective requires you to clear.\n49. When a duplicated deck uses suit-matching ace-up foundations as the main sink, foundation capacity must scale with the number of copies, not just the number of suits. If the sink can only absorb part of the deck, the game may produce lots of legal motion and still be fundamentally unwinnable.\n50. When a solitaire file fails to compile due to deck mismatch, prefer the smallest count correction that preserves the intended flow of cards. Restoring exact card totals without simultaneously redesigning the move graph makes later playtest results much easier to interpret.\n51. When a win condition depends on emptying the tableau via foundations, foundation capacity must match the number of full suit sequences present in the deck. Duplicate suits or duplicate decks often require duplicated completion piles; otherwise the game can be mathematically unwinnable no matter how much tableau mobility exists.\n52. When a solitaire variant shows high search activity but zero wins, first strengthen the irreversible progress path rather than adding more tableau movement. Incremental sinks that accept ordinary exposed cards usually convert churn into solvable structure better than all-at-once completion gates.\n53. When a solitaire variant isn't even compiling, the best next step is usually a minimal structural repair that preserves pile roles and card flow; tuning difficulty before you can observe real play data is mostly guesswork.\n54. When