pile type already exists, converting it into a proper completion sink is usually a cleaner fix than adding more rearrangement rules.\n60. When a game has lots of legal motion, high card usage, and repeated search-cap failures, the problem is often not mobility but lack of enough irreversible sink capacity. Adding or parallelizing completion piles can improve solvability more cleanly than making tableau moves even freer.\n61. When a compact solitaire has many 0-move or 1-move proven losses, the first fix is often not more movement rules but more starting contact: exposing more initial cards can dramatically improve solvability without changing the game\u2019s core objective or sink structure.\n\nIn reduced-deck, single-suit games, standard tableau relations are often already permissive enough; if play is still dead, hidden information and blocked exposure are likelier culprits than insufficient move freedom.\n62. When a solitaire file fails to compile for unused cards, fix the arithmetic first with the smallest count change that preserves the intended pile roles. Restoring a runnable baseline is more informative than redesigning rules before you can observe actual play.\n63. When a solitaire generates many long, unresolved searches, the usual culprit is not too little movement but too little irreversible progress. A good repair is to make the existing sink accept the kinds of local structures the tableau naturally creates, so reorganization steadily converts into cards leaving play instead of circulating forever.\n64. When a duplicated deck uses suit-specific foundations, strict ace-up building can underprovide sink access even if tableau mobility is high. Adding a narrowly targeted incremental sink relation\u2014such as permitting duplicates of the current foundation rank\u2014can reduce search churn by turning more exposed cards into irreversible progress without broadly loosening the tableau.\n65. When a solitaire has many legal rearrangements but no wins, look first for pile types that absorb cards without clearly advancing the objective. Converting those areas into irreversible progress sinks often helps more than adding yet more mobility, especially in double-deck games where foundation capacity is a common hidden bottleneck.\n66. When a game has some wins but many long search-cap losses, the problem is often not total lack of mobility but too little effective workspace relative to the stock. Expanding tableau capacity slightly\u2014while preserving the main sink and move logic\u2014can improve both opening contact and meaningful decision-making without flattening the game into triviality.\n67. When a solitaire variant fails before simulation, fix exact card accounting and other structural validity issues with the smallest possible change first. A runnable but imperfect design teaches more than a larger speculative rebalance that still obscures whether the core rules even function.\n68. When a solitaire already has a one-way accumulation area, first try aligning victory and progress around that sink instead of adding more mobility elsewhere. A rearrangement game without an irreversible win path tends to produce lots of search and pile usage but no actual wins.\n69. When a solitaire produces many legal moves but no wins, look for a mismatch between the way cards are organized in the tableau and the way permanent progress is scored. Making the sink accept cards in the same structural direction the workspace builds often improves solvability more than adding yet more mobility.\n70. When a solitaire already uses most cards and piles but loses mainly by hitting search limits, the problem is often excessive reversible freedom rather than insufficient mobility. Tightening the cheapest tableau move to empty spaces\u2014especially by rank-gating it\u2014can improve decision quality and search tractability without rebuilding the whole game.\n71. When a solitaire produces long, non-exhaustive losses with moderate card usage, the bottleneck is often not mobility but lack of incremental irreversible progress. Replacing an all-at-once completion gate with stepwise sink building can reduce churn while preserving the need to organize cards.\n72. When a solitaire already has high card/pile usage but its losses come from hitting search limits, the problem is often excessive reversible mobility rather than lack of access. Tightening empty-pile reuse is a good first lever: it preserves tactical relocation while cutting down on churn and making irreversible progress easier to prioritize.\n73. When a solitaire has a valid end sink but many exhausted 0-move starts, improve opening exposure before changing the core build rule. A small increase in visible cards often rescues dead deals while preserving the game\u2019s identity better than adding broad new mobility.\n74. When a solitaire generates long, high-usage losses instead of quick exhausted ones, the problem is often reversible circulation rather than lack of mobility. In that situation, improve the main organization/progress route and constrain the broadest temporary-storage loop before adding still more movement.\n75. When a solitaire file does not compile because card totals are short, prefer the smallest repair that preserves the intended pile roles and move graph; you need a valid runnable baseline before difficulty tuning means anything. In empty-the-tableau games, adding missing cards to an existing tableau/work pile is usually less disruptive than inventing a new sink or changing victory conditions prematurely.\n76. When a solitaire file fails to compile because cards are unassigned, fix deck accounting with the smallest role-preserving change first. A minimal count repair keeps the move graph interpretable, so later playtest data reflects gameplay problems rather than setup noise.\n77. When a solitaire fails to compile because the initial layout underuses the deck, prefer the smallest count adjustment that preserves the existing pile roles and move graph. A simple accounting fix can also improve opening contact if the extra cards are added to already-active tableau piles rather than inventing new subsystems.\n78. If a solitaire already has a one-way receiving area, first try aligning victory to that area before adding more mobility elsewhere. Losses with moderate pile usage but no wins often come from \u201crearrangement without resolution\u201d: the game needs a sink that accepts ordinary progress, not just more tableau movement.\n79. When a solitaire has high move availability but zero wins, first check whether its irreversible progress path matches the victory condition. Rearrangement-heavy tableau rules need a sink that rewards the same structure players are asked to build; otherwise the game produces churn instead of progress.\n80. When a game already has a one-way receiving pile, first ask whether it should be the victory destination rather than adding more mobility elsewhere. If the win condition demands tableau piles to empty, the sink that absorbs cards must be part of the win path; otherwise the design can become provably unwinnable even with legal moves available.\n81. When a game uses duplicated suits or multiple parallel suit-runs, the number and capacity pattern of completion piles must scale with that duplication. If the sink only supports one run per suit while the deck contains two, many deals become unwinnable regardless of how much tableau movement is allowed.\n82. When a solitaire layout has an emptying win condition, fixing deck accounting should usually preserve and complete the intended card-flow pattern rather than stuffing leftover cards into arbitrary piles. A stock/draw pile is often the smallest valid repair when the existing tableau already implies a Klondike-style expose \u2192 rearrange \u2192 advance structure.\n83. When a reduced-suit or duplicated deck uses suit-matching foundations, foundation count is a real capacity constraint, not just cosmetic structure. If some deals are provably unwinnable despite healthy tableau mobility, adding parallel sink lanes can improve solvability more cleanly than making the tableau looser.\n84. When a design already has a one-way receiving pile that represents progress, first align victory with that sink instead of demanding some separate source pile state the language cannot express cleanly. Minimal compile fixes are best when they also clarify the intended card-flow: workspace empties into sink.\n85. When victory requires tableau piles to empty into suit-built sinks, sink parallelism must match the deck\u2019s duplication structure; otherwise local mobility may exist, but the game is globally unwinnable. If a one-way progress area already exists, expanding its capacity is often a cleaner fix than adding new movement rules.\n86. When a solitaire variant fails to compile because not all cards are placed, fix deck accounting before judging balance or solvability. The best first repair is usually the smallest count adjustment that preserves the intended pile roles and card flow, so later playtest results reflect the design rather than a rewritten setup.\n87. When a game already has an irreversible sink, first align victory with that sink before adding more mechanics. Requiring untouched source piles to empty can create unwinnable bookkeeping goals that don\u2019t support the real play pattern.\n88. When a solitaire already has a viable win path but many losses come from search-cap churn, tighten the loosest reversible holding area before adding new mobility. A helper pile that accepts almost anything often explodes the state space; rank-gating entry can preserve decision value while making progress easier for both players and solvers to recognize.\n89. When a solitaire sometimes wins cleanly but other deals are proven unwinnable after only a handful of explored moves, the first suspect is the opening exposure, not the midgame rules. A small increase in visible starting cards can rescue dead starts while preserving the game\u2019s existing structure and decision space.\n90. When a game uses duplicated suits, completion sinks must be duplicated too; otherwise the player may have abundant legal movement but no way to absorb the full deck into irreversible progress.\n\nIf simulations consistently hit search caps with high card/pile usage, check for a structural capacity mismatch before adding more mobility\u2014the problem is often that the win path cannot hold everything the game asks the player to clear.\n91. When a rules engine forbids multiple move definitions for the same source/destination pair, merge them into one condition tree instead of adding parallel exceptions. Minimal compile repairs preserve the game\u2019s underlying card flow, making later balance feedback much easier to interpret.\n92. If a win condition requires tableau piles to empty, any one-way destination piles must be able to serve as a full, reachable sink for the deck. Pre-populating foundation-like piles with random cards often makes large parts of the deck permanently unplaceable unless those piles\u2019 build rules are designed around that exact setup.\n93. When a solitaire already wins often but occasional losses come from hitting a search cap with high card usage, the problem is often reversible freedom rather than lack of access. Tightening the noisiest generic move\u2014especially unrestricted single-card moves to empty spaces\u2014can reduce churn while preserving meaningful mobility through more structured stack moves.\n94. When a solitaire file fails to compile for unused cards, fix deck accounting with the smallest role-preserving adjustment first; a valid baseline is more informative than premature rule tuning. When possible, add missing cards to an existing pile type rather than introducing new mechanics before you have playtest data.\n95. When a game uses duplicated suit sets, its completion sinks need matching parallel capacity; otherwise the design can generate lots of legal motion but no realistic path to absorb all cards. Expanding an existing irreversible sink is often a better first fix than adding more tableau freedom.\n96. When a solitaire generates lots of movement but no wins, first check whether irreversible sink capacity and parallelism match the deck\u2019s duplication. Extra tableau freedom often increases churn, while adding enough completion lanes can turn existing organization into actual progress.\n97. When a win condition depends on emptying active piles into foundations, foundation capacity has to match the deck\u2019s duplicated suits and total card count; otherwise the game can be unwinnable regardless of local mobility. Also, making progress sinks irreversible is often a cleaner first repair than adding more tableau motion, because it reduces churn while clarifying the path to victory.\n98. If a win condition asks tableau piles to empty, the design must provide enough irreversible sink capacity to absorb the whole deck. In reduced-suit decks especially, suit-restricted foundations that work in full decks may become mathematically too narrow unless the sink count or matching rule is adjusted to fit the card set.\n99. If a game already has a one-way receiving pile, first make that pile a practical irreversible sink before adding new subsystems. Search-cap losses with high pile usage often mean the player can rearrange freely but lacks a sink that accepts progress in small, reachable steps.\n100. When a multi-deck solitaire has an emptying win condition and uses foundations as its only irreversible sink, too few valid foundation starts can bottleneck the whole design even if tableau mobility is high. Widening the sink\u2019s entry condition slightly is often a better first fix than adding more free movement, because it converts existing organization into lasting progress instead of more churn.\n\nProduce the next version of skill.md: rewrite it as a whole, integrating what's genuinely new or well-supported from the insights above into the existing document rather than appending to it. Keep the entire document to at most 20 bullet points total, organized under a small number of clear headers. End your response with the complete new skill.md in a single fenced code block tagged `skill`, as the last thing in your response."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "```skill\n# skill.md\n\n## Establish a valid, playable core\n- Treat compile failures, bad deck accounting, invalid references, impossible predicates, and overlapping move-definition conflicts as first-order design failures. Fix the smallest structural issue that restores a runnable game before tuning difficulty.\n- Prefer minimal, informative repairs: preserve existing pile roles, card flow, and move graph where possible; restore exact card totals; and re-check that every rule, sink, and win-condition target is valid in context.\n- Validate rule/setup compatibility early. Build and sink predicates must match the actual deck structure: reduced suits, duplicated suits, multiple deck copies, prefilled receiving piles, or collapsed color distributions can make standard-looking tableau or foundation rules impossible, trivial, or too narrow.\n- Repair opening contact before balancing challenge. Universal 0-move starts, tiny exhausted searches, or populated pile types with no legal role usually mean the opening layout or move graph lacks a reachable first progress route.\n\n## Align sinks, victory, and deck structure\n- If the win condition requires active piles to empty, the design needs at least one reachable, irreversible sink that the objective explicitly rewards. Rearrangement alone cannot satisfy an emptying goal.\n- Sink capacity and parallelism must match the deck\u2019s real completion structure. Duplicated suits, repeated suit-runs, multiple deck copies, reduced-suit decks, or partially occupied receiving piles can make a familiar foundation system mathematically unable to absorb the full deck.\n- Match the sink rule to the deck, not to convention. When standard same-suit ace-up foundations underfit the card set, adjust sink count, acceptance classes, or progression pattern so ordinary exposed cards can become durable progress.\n- Prefer incremental sinks over all-at-once completion gates. Stepwise advancement usually converts local organization into steady wins more reliably than requiring fully assembled runs before any irreversible progress is scored.\n- When a one-way receiving or accumulation area already exists, first consider making it the main victory sink, expanding its capacity, or aligning the win condition to it before adding new subsystems or more tableau freedom.\n- If a pile can receive cards but not meaningfully release them, it should either represent rewarded completion or be removed/reworked. One-way piles outside the win path often create dead sinks, blockers, or unwinnable bookkeeping goals.\n- Keep main progress sinks as irreversible as possible. Allow backflow only for a specific strategic purpose; otherwise it usually increases churn more than it adds meaningful decisions.\n\n## Shape mobility to support progress\n- Distinguish deadness from churn. Near-zero use and tiny exhausted searches usually indicate missing opening contact or an unreachable sink; high move counts, broad usage, and search-cap losses usually indicate reversible motion without enough rewarded progress.\n- When movement is plentiful but wins are absent, improve the sink before adding mobility: make progress reachable, incremental, and large enough to absorb the deck under the actual rules.\n- Favor movement rules that organize cards in the same direction the sink rewards. Tableau builds and transfers should prepare ordinary exposed cards for advancement, not create free circulation disconnected from victory.\n- Constrain the broadest reversible routes before adding new ones. Unrestricted empty-space fills, overly permissive helper piles, and generic single-card shuffling are common sources of search churn.\n- Prefer constrained empty-pile rules over unrestricted ones. Rank-gated, single-card, or otherwise narrow access to empties often rescues dead starts while preserving planning value and reducing solver noise.\n- Broad holding areas are risky. Helper piles should be clearly temporary and clearable, or else converted into structured progress zones.\n\n## Tune openings and pacing with small changes\n- For frequent 0- or 1-move losses in an otherwise coherent game, first improve opening contact with the smallest compatible change: slightly more starting exposure, a narrowly available workspace destination, or a modest increase in effective tableau capacity.\n- If some deals already win cleanly but others die almost immediately, suspect blocked information or cramped early workspace before rewriting the core build rules.\n- When a stock or deal pile feeds cards into active piles, it should participate in the main progress route rather than act as delayed blockage. Pacing elements work best when they expose or supply cards that can realistically reach the sink.\n\n## Express rules cleanly\n- Keep move definitions compact and non-overlapping. If the engine forbids multiple rules for the same source/destination pair, merge them into one coherent condition tree instead of scattering exceptions.\n```"}]